{"id":"h062","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"Find the fraction of the standard solar flux reaching the Earth (about 1000 W/m^2) available to a solar collector lying flat on the Earth’s surface at Miami (latitude 26°N) at noon on the winter solstice.\nIs the correct answer \"0.615\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct fraction is about 0.65, not 0.615.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The Sun's declination at the winter solstice is about -23.44°.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"At noon at 26°N the zenith angle is 26° + 23.44° = 49.44°.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"A flat collector receives the standard flux times the cosine of the angle between sunlight and the surface normal.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"cos(49.44°) is approximately 0.650, giving about 650 W/m².","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Getting 0.615 would require a zenith angle near 52°, which the given latitude and declination do not provide.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.7,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Atmospheric absorption could reduce flux further, but no extra attenuation is intended since the problem asks only for the geometric factor.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-0.4,"theta":1.0996,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The result is insensitive to rounding; using 23.5° gives 0.649.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.6,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Nothing in the problem clearly supports the value 0.615.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i"